# OpenCovers — environment variables for local development.
#
#   cp .env.example .env     (then uncomment only what you need to override)
#
# Local development works with NO .env at all: every variable below has a default
# for localhost (BETTER_AUTH_SECRET's is public: fine locally, never elsewhere). The API loads ../../.env automatically when the file
# exists (see the `dev`, `start` and `db:migrate` scripts in apps/api).
#
# Every line is commented out on purpose. The code reads `process.env.X ?? default`,
# so an EMPTY value (`PORT=`) would override the default with an empty string.
# Uncomment a line only when you give it a real value. Never commit a real .env.
#
# For the production Docker stack use .env.prod.example instead (compose.prod.yaml).

# --- API (apps/api) ----------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string for the dev database.
# Default: postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/opencovers
# Comes from `docker compose up -d` (compose.yaml) or your own PostgreSQL 16.
# DATABASE_URL=

# Port the API listens on. Default: 3000.
# The staff app dev server proxies /api and /v1 to http://localhost:3000.
# PORT=

# Secret that signs sessions (Better Auth). Default: a fixed development value that
# is public in the source, so anyone can forge sessions if it is left in place.
# REQUIRED in any shared or production environment. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Who may create an account: open, first-user or closed. Default: open, so local
# development needs nothing. An unknown value stops the API at startup.
# first-user lets the first account sign up and refuses every later one. closed refuses all.
# compose.prod.yaml defaults to first-user. See docs/adr/0005-signup-control.md.
# SIGNUP=

# Rate limits on sign-in, sign-up and invitation redemption. See docs/adr/0007-rate-limiting.md.
# RATE_LIMIT is on or off. Default: on when NODE_ENV=production, off otherwise, so local development
# needs nothing. RATE_LIMIT=on or off always wins. Outside Docker, set NODE_ENV=production or RATE_LIMIT=on.
# TRUST_PROXY is true or false. Default: false, which uses the socket address and ignores
# X-Forwarded-For. Set true only when a reverse proxy is the sole way to reach the API.
# The three budgets read max/window, with a window in s, m or h. A bad value stops the API at startup.
#   RATE_LIMIT_AUTH        default 30/15m   sign-in and other writes under /api/auth
#   RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP      default 5/1h     sign-up
#   RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION  default 10/15m   invitation redemption and sign-up with an invitation token
# RATE_LIMIT=
# TRUST_PROXY=
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH=
# RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP=
# RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION=

# Public URL of the API as users reach it. Default: http://localhost:3000
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to sign in / call the API.
# Default: the local dev origins. Set it to your public URL when you deploy.
# TRUSTED_ORIGINS=

# Email confirmations through Resend. Optional: with these unset, no email is sent.
# RESEND_API_KEY is created at https://resend.com/api-keys (a secret).
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL is a sender on a domain you verified in Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=

# --- Tests (apps/api) --------------------------------------------------------

# Database the integration tests wipe and use. Default:
# postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/opencovers_test
# Never point it at data you care about. compose.yaml creates opencovers_test.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# Development switch for webhooks (apps/api/src/lib/webhooks.ts). Only the exact
# string `true` turns it on; then webhooks may target plain http and private
# addresses, and the API logs a warning at startup. The webhook e2e test sets it.
# Never enable it in a shared or production environment.
# WEBHOOK_ALLOW_INSECURE=

# --- MCP server (apps/mcp) ---------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the API the MCP server talks to. Default: http://localhost:3000
# OPENCOVERS_BASE_URL=

# Agent key the MCP server authenticates with (a secret). Create it in the staff
# app under Setup → API keys (venue admins only). It is usually set in the MCP
# host's config (Claude Desktop, Cursor), not here.
# OPENCOVERS_API_KEY=
